  "description": "OpenStreetMap is a global collaborative map database built by volunteers, holding roads, buildings, amenities, public spaces and many other features as nodes, ways and relations with key=value tags. It is run by the OpenStreetMap Foundation and edited continuously by its contributors; the project began in London. Buildings are tagged with building=* (198 documented values), and taginfo counted about 710 million objects with a building tag on the day read, almost all as footprint ways; functions such as hospitals, schools or shelters are tagged separately with amenity=* and similar keys. Coverage is worldwide but uneven, depending on local mapping and imports. Access: weekly planet files (166 GB XML, 88 GB PBF, a full history file and changesets), the editing API for small bounding boxes, and many third-party extracts. Licence: Open Database License for data published after 12 September 2012, with attribution and share-alike; free. Caveats: completeness and attribute detail vary strongly by place, and building use tags do not always reflect current function. Simulation uses: building and facility exposure, service access and evacuation routing, and network models built from the road graph. Related records: HOT country exports on HDX, OpenBuildingMap and OSMnx.",
